<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Commandancy General of the Internal Provinces</h1><p class="st-meta">1776 CE – 1821 CE · capital Arizpe; later Chihuahua</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Military command created by Bourbon Spain to defend the vast northern frontier of New Spain, from Texas and New Mexico to Sonora and the Californias.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Under the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-galvez">José de Gálvez</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III</a> separated the northern provinces of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a>'s direct control in 1776 to strengthen frontier defence against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teodoro-de-croix">Teodoro de Croix</a> became the first commandant general, with his seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizpe">Arizpe</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonora">Sonora</a>, later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chihuahua-city">Chihuahua</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The commandant general held military, political and judicial authority over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nueva-vizcaya">Nueva Vizcaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonora">Sonora</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinaloa">Sinaloa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-californias">Californias</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">New Mexico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coahuila">Coahuila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, and at times <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuevo-leon">Nuevo León</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nuevo-santander">Nuevo Santander</a>. The command rested on a chain of presidios, mounted soldiers and alliances or peace treaties with indigenous groups, notably the 1786 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> peace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>. It was repeatedly reorganised, divided into eastern and western commands and placed back under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The command continued, in eastern and western halves after 1813, until Mexican independence in 1821. Its territories became states and territories of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a>, and much of its north, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">New Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> in 1848.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/commandancy-general-of-the-internal-provinces.jpg" alt="Commandancy General of the Internal Provinces" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ningyou. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cross_of_Burgundy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1776 CE – 1821 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arizpe; later Chihuahua</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Teodoro de Croix, Jacobo Ugarte y Loyola, Pedro de Nava</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>, <a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Provincias Internas, Internal Provinces</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1798&amp;focus=commandancy-general-of-the-internal-provinces" aria-label="Commandancy General of the Internal Provinces on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1822 1071 640 5